Squid in containers
===================

The core squid package contains the proxy and its disk, file logging, and
certificate-generation helpers. It has no shell scripts or RPM scriptlets.
The optional squid-helpers package contains authentication, external ACL,
rewrite, and database logging helpers, including their script interpreters.

Run /usr/bin/squid -N -f /etc/squid/squid.conf as the container process.
The -N option keeps Squid in the foreground without a service manager.

For container logs, set these directives in squid.conf:

    access_log stdio:/dev/stdout
    cache_log /dev/stderr
    pid_filename none

The image must provide the squid user and group, both with ID 23, as declared
in /usr/lib/sysusers.d/squid.conf. Run Squid as that user. Mount writable
directories at /var/log/squid and /var/spool/squid with suitable ownership.

If the configuration enables a disk cache, initialize its directories with
/usr/bin/squid -N -z -f /etc/squid/squid.conf before the main process starts.
Use a separate container initialization step; no shell wrapper is required.

The package does not include systemd, NetworkManager, Apache, or logrotate
integration. The container runtime controls process startup and log rotation.
